messages:
julio_customer: Hey team — we've started seeing a pile of 429s for customers hitting the redwood-alpha-extended-64k model. Happens during a nightly batch around 02:00 UTC, bursty. Can someone help?

emma_support: sorry you're hitting this, julio. can you paste a sample request or how you're batching? also which SDK/version and auth method?

julio_customer: thanks. we use our node service (axios) to stream prompts to /v1/generate. single large request ~90k tokens in context (we merge chat history + long doc). Not streaming the tokens back, just full response. no retries. tokenization looks right. auth via API key. SDK is homemade.

ciara_eng: 90k context on redwood-alpha-extended-64k — that's above the model's nominal context length. that will trigger context-length throttling or early 429s if the runtime rejects pre-flight. do you have server logs showing response body? our 429 responses include the quota bucket and rate window usually.

julio_customer: sample curl we used: ```
curl -i -X POST https://api.redwood.ai/v1/generate \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $API_KEY" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"model":"redwood-alpha-extended-64k","input":"<big payload>","max_tokens":2000}'
```

julio_customer: response headers: status 429, body: {"error":"rate_limited","message":"model rate limit exceeded","meta":{"model":"redwood-alpha-extended-64k","code":"model_rpm_exceeded"}}

emma_support: good details. that meta.code suggests per-model RPM/TPM cap hit rather than auth. can you confirm request rate over the window? e.g., how many requests per minute during that 02:00 spike?

julio_customer: short spike: ~250 req/min for 2 minutes, then back to 40 req/min. average outside window is 20/min.

allen_sre: quick note: large context requests also take longer to process -> they tie up concurrency slots and can push you into per-model concurrency limits which then result in 429s upstream. do you have traces for request latency?

julio_customer: p95 processing time reported by our client ~3.2s for the small ones, but these large-context ones take 12-18s.

ciara_eng: ok that aligns. here's how our limits interplay: per-model RPM/TPM sets throughput across customers; max concurrency is number of in-flight requests allowed for that model variant; huge context = longer hold time = fewer requests served per minute => if you burst request rate it exhausts both RPM and concurrency. runtime will prefer to decline with 429s tagged model_rpm_exceeded or model_concurrency_exceeded.

marta_pm: adding product context: redwood-alpha-extended-64k has a documented max context of 64k; anything beyond is unsupported and will either be truncated or rejected depending on deployment flags. hitting 90k explains heuristics kicking in. we typically recommend chunking the doc or using our embeddings pipeline to pre-filter.

julio_customer: we didn't realize it was strictly enforced. i thought it would silently truncate. the SDK we're using doesn't check before send. any quick mitigation?

ciara_eng: short-term: reduce parallelism and backoff. implement these repro steps: 1) batch requests so you never exceed ~50 req/min for that model during your window; 2) limit concurrency to 8 in-flight for that model variant; 3) split context into 64k windows or summarize to fit. Also add exponential backoff with jitter on 429s.

ciara_eng: here's a suggested client-side snippet (pseudo): ```
async function sendWithConcurrency(queue, maxConcurrent=8) {
  // simple semaphore implementation
}
async function callRedwood(payload) {
  if (payload.tokens > 64000) {
    // do chunk/summarize before calling
  }
  try {
    await api.generate(payload)
  } catch (e) {
    if (e.status === 429) {
      await backoffAndRetry(e)
    }
  }
}
```

rate-limiter-bot: Observed metrics for model redwood-alpha-extended-64k during 2024-06-01T02:00Z: RPM window peak: 310rpm, sustained 160rpm for 3min. Max concurrent slots used: 42/40 -> concurrency throttles engaged. Token-time per request averaged 14s.

allen_sre: rate-limiter-bot confirms concurrency oversubscription (used 42 while limit 40) — that causes queueing and eventual 429s. runtime uses a fast-fail policy when in-flight > concurrency+queue threshold to preserve latency SLOs.

emma_support: i've escalated to infra team to see if any misrouting increased your observed rate. ciara, allen — any notes to share with customer?

ciara_eng: we'll follow up with: 1) confirm model variant's exact RPM/TPM and concurrency cap for their org (some orgs have different reserved tiers); 2) check whether the customer's requests were routed to a lower-tier pool accidentally; 3) recommend using a smaller context variant or our streaming call with prefix caching to reduce cost.

julio_customer: thanks. for the immediate fix we'll throttle our job and implement chunking. can you pls tell me where to find the per-model limit numbers so we can programmatically adapt?

emma_support: docs live at https://docs.redwood.ai/rate-limits (section: "Per-model limits and throttling"). i'll DM a quick summary of your org's limits. also include headers we return on 429: X-Redwood-Model, X-Redwood-Quota-Bucket, Retry-After when applicable.

marta_pm: also note: if you have a Dedicated plan, we can provision higher concurrency and throughput for specific models. reach out to sales if you want a stability SLA for these nightly batch windows. :shield:

julio_customer: appreciated. i'll implement the changes and reopen if we still see failures. thanks all :thumbsup:

emma_support: closing the loop — i'll put a follow-up ticket with infra and cc you, julio. feel free to ping if you want a quick pairing to implement client-side concurrency controls.
